WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.776
A second reviewer: fresh sessions, subagents, other models.

2
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:09.432
An independent reviewer catches what the author missed, and with agents you can get one cheaply.

3
00:00:09.712 --> 00:00:12.485
The best-practices page describes three ways.

4
00:00:12.765 --> 00:00:22.344
Claude Code’s /code-review reviews the diff in a fresh subagent that never saw your conversation, so it isn’t swayed by the reasoning that produced the code.

5
00:00:22.624 --> 00:00:29.344
A custom read-only reviewer subagent (lesson 5) does the same with your own brief, and can’t edit anything.

6
00:00:29.624 --> 00:00:36.493
Or open a second session, have it review the files, and carry the findings back yourself: the writer-reviewer pattern.

7
00:00:36.773 --> 00:00:40.016
Three things make model review useful rather than noisy.

8
00:00:40.296 --> 00:00:46.781
Give it the spec, and ask it to check that every requirement is implemented and each listed edge case has a test.

9
00:00:47.061 --> 00:00:52.096
Narrow the question to correctness and the checklist items above, and tell it to skip style.

10
00:00:52.376 --> 00:01:02.936
The docs warn that a reviewer asked to find problems will always find something, and fixing every finding leads to needless abstraction and tests for impossible cases.

11
00:01:03.216 --> 00:01:14.096
Make it independent: a fresh context at minimum, and for high-stakes changes, consider a different model family, whose errors are less likely to be correlated with the author’s.

12
00:01:14.376 --> 00:01:26.429
Then remember what Studio 3 established about model judges: they have biases, they agree with people often but not always, and they need calibration against human labels before you rely on them.

13
00:01:26.709 --> 00:01:30.741
A model reviewer is a filter that raises issues for you to judge.

14
00:01:31.021 --> 00:01:32.451
It is not the approval.

15
00:01:32.731 --> 00:01:43.504
The best-practices page’s own table puts “a person who approves the merge” and branch protection in the deterministic column; the model’s summary of its work is in the other one.
